<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
Fast DDS profile for cmd_vel_watchdog.py on the HOST side. Port 42102.

Why a second file and a second port rather than reusing host_tcp_server.xml.

In this topology the guest can only dial out, so a host participant is reachable
from the guest only if it owns a TCP acceptor. A listening port has exactly one
owner: whichever participant binds it first wins, and every later one logs

    TCPTransport Error binding at port: (42100) with msg: bind: Address already
    in use -> Function create_acceptor_socket

and then runs with no acceptor at all, discoverable but unreachable. Measured
2026-08-17: with the decimator sharing host_tcp_server.xml, the guest listed
/clock and never received a sample, while the decimator itself was forwarding
normally (in 1903, out 351).

So the rule for this design is one listening port per host participant that has
to reach the guest, and a matching entry in the guest's initialPeersList:

    42100  parameter_bridge   /scan /odom /tf, and /cmd_vel inbound
    42101  clock_decimator    /clock
    42102  cmd_vel_watchdog   /cmd_vel inbound (this file)

Measured 2026-08-19, the tb4 G5 run, which is why this file exists: with the
watchdog sharing host_tcp_server.xml, `artifacts/watchdog-20260819-133815.log`
opens with exactly the error quoted above for port 42100, so the watchdog ran the
whole drive with no acceptor. It therefore saw no /cmd_vel at all, and since it
zeroes after 2.0 s of silence, the only reason it did not fight Nav2 is that its
own zeros could not reach the bridge either. A silent no-op in both directions.
That trap is in this rig's own README "Do not" table; the design note above named
42102 as the fix and the runner kept using 42100.

Selected per process by environment, not by profile name:
    FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=/work/config/host_cmdvel_server.xml
Node-name-based profile lookup would work too, but an env var is per-process and
leaves no doubt about which participant got which port.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>

    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>cmdvel_server</transport_id>
        <type>TCPv4</type>

        <listening_ports>
          <port>42102</port>
        </listening_ports>

        <!-- Same reason as host_tcp_server.xml: without it the announced locators
             come from this host's own interfaces, none of which SLIRP routes from
             the guest, and the link comes up and delivers nothing. -->
        <wan_addr>10.0.2.2</wan_addr>

        <enable_tcp_nodelay>true</enable_tcp_nodelay>
        <keep_alive_frequency_ms>30000</keep_alive_frequency_ms>
        <keep_alive_timeout_ms>120000</keep_alive_timeout_ms>
        <calculate_crc>false</calculate_crc>
        <check_crc>false</check_crc>
        <max_logical_port>200</max_logical_port>
        <logical_port_range>50</logical_port_range>
        <maxMessageSize>65000</maxMessageSize>
        <sendBufferSize>1048576</sendBufferSize>
        <receiveBufferSize>1048576</receiveBufferSize>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="vela_host_cmdvel" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>cmdvel_server</transport_id>
        </userTransports>

        <!-- true, and required here for the same reason as the decimator: this
             node PUBLISHES /cmd_vel to parameter_bridge, which is another host
             process. With builtin transports off it would have no SHM and no UDP,
             so its zero-velocity command could never reach the bridge and the
             watchdog would be a no-op against a latched DiffDrive. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>true</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>

    <!-- A Twist is 48 bytes, so fragmentation is not the concern it is on
         host_tcp_server.xml. Kept asynchronous anyway so every host writer behaves
         the same way. -->
    <data_writer profile_name="vela_cmdvel_async" is_default_profile="true">
      <qos>
        <publishMode>
          <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
        </publishMode>
      </qos>
    </data_writer>

  </profiles>
</dds>
